          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Be the first player to shed all cards from hand; if you cannot win, minimize cards left when another player goes out.',
          gameplay: 'Play proceeds in tricks. A lead may be a legal ordinary combination or bomb. Each following play must be the same combination type and exactly one rank higher, or a permitted special response such as a two-set on singles/pairs, or a bomb. Players may pass. When all but one player pass, the trick ends; players draw from the talon starting with the last player to play, then that player leads the next trick.',
          keyRules: [
            'Singles, pairs, sequences, and double-sequences are ordinary combinations; responses must usually be exactly one rank higher.',
            'Twos are highest in singles and pairs but cannot be used inside sequences or double-sequences.',
            'Bombs (triples, four-of-a-kind, and double-joker bomb) can beat ordinary combinations, with larger bomb classes beating smaller ones.',
            'Jokers are wild in combinations but cannot normally be played as single cards unless it is your only remaining card when leading.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Preserve flexible middle ranks so you can answer exact one-step climbs instead of passing too early.',
          intermediate: 'Track talon depth and trick timing: winning a trick near the end of talon can secure lead control without feeding opponents extra draws.',
          advanced: 'Use bombs as tempo tools rather than panic plays; forcing passes at the right moment can create a clean lead where you unload multiple combinations in sequence.',
          tips: [
            'Avoid spending twos too early unless they secure critical lead control.',
            'Plan around exact-step responses: a gap in rank often means forced pass even with strong cards.',
            'Count likely bomb density from exposed plays before committing a high ordinary combination.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
